nodes.statement:
Namely, we consider a dispatching algorithm that uses an integral parameter $\Delta \geq 1$ and a threshold $\ell_n \in \mathbbm{N}$ to distribute the incoming tasks among the $n$ server pools. This algorithm aims at maintaining the number of tasks at each server pool between $\ell_n$ and $h_n \vcentcolon= \ell_n + \Delta$ by dispatching every incoming task as follows.
\begin{itemize}
	\item If some server pool has strictly less than $\ell_n$ tasks right before the arrival, then the new task is sent to a server pool having strictly less than $\ell_n$ tasks, chosen uniformly at random.

	\item If all server pools have at least $\ell_n$ tasks right before the arrival, and some server pool has strictly less than $h_n$ tasks, then the new task is sent to a server pool chosen uniformly at random among those having at least $\ell_n$ and strictly less than $h_n$ tasks.

	\item If all server pools have at least $h_n$ tasks right before the arrival, then the new task is sent to a server pool having at least $h_n$ tasks, chosen uniformly at random.
\end{itemize}

This compromise is attained by increasing $\Delta$ and setting
\begin{equation*}
	\ell_n(t) = \floor{\frac{N_n(t)}{n}}_\Delta \vcentcolon= \max \set{k \Delta}{k \Delta \leq \frac{N_n(t)}{n}\ \text{and}\ k \in \mathbbm{Z}} \quad \text{for all} \quad t.
\end{equation*}

An alternative learning scheme for finding the optimal threshold value consists of adjusting the threshold in the appropriate direction whenever the objective of the dispatching rule is violated. This more refined scheme is parameterized by $\alpha_n \in (0, 1)$ and uses the same state information as the dispatching rule. In this scheme, the time-dependent threshold takes values on the non-negative multiples of $\Delta$ and is adjusted in steps of $\Delta$ units at certain arrival epochs. Specifically, when an arrival occurs, the refined learning scheme acts under the following circumstances.
\begin{itemize}
	\item If the fraction of server pools having at least $\ell_n$ tasks is smaller than or equal to $\alpha_n$ right before the arrival, then the threshold is decreased $\Delta$ units once the new task has been dispatched.

	\item If all the server pools have at least $\ell_n$ tasks and the number of server pools with at least $h_n$ tasks is larger than or equal to $n - 1$ right before the arrival, then the threshold is increased $\Delta$ units right after dispatching the task that has just arrived.
\end{itemize}

Note that the case $r = 1$ corresponds to exponentially distributed service times and that the mean service time of a task is
\begin{equation*}
	\frac{1}{\mu} = \sum_{i = 1}^r (1 - p_i) \left(\prod_{j = 1}^{i - 1} p_j\right) \sum_{k = 1}^i \frac{1}{\mu_k}.
\end{equation*}

The occupancy process $s_n$ takes values in
\begin{equation*}
	S \vcentcolon= \set{s \in [0, 1]^{\mathbbm{N}^r}}{\sum_{j_1, \dots, j_r \in \mathbbm{N}} s(j_1, \dots, j_r) = 1} \subset \mathbbm{R}^{\mathbbm{N}^r},
\end{equation*}
while the process $q_n$ takes values in
\begin{equation*}
	Q \vcentcolon= \set{q \in [0, 1]^{\mathbbm{N}}}{q(i + 1) \leq q(i) \leq q(0) = 1\ \text{for all}\ i \geq 0} \subset \mathbbm{R}^{\mathbbm{N}}.
\end{equation*}

Consider the product topology in $\mathbbm{R}^{\mathbbm{N}^r}$ and assume that there exists $s(0) \in S$ such that
\begin{equation} \lim_{n \to \infty} s_n(0) = s(0) \quad \text{almost surely};
\end{equation}
here $s_n(0)$ is the occupancy process evaluated at time zero and $s(0)$ is a constant that describes the limiting occupancy state of the system.

Let $G_m$ denote the complementary cumulative distribution function of the residual service time of a task in phase $m$, and consider the function $\map{u}{[0, T]}{[0, \infty)}$ such that
\begin{equation} u^m(0) \vcentcolon= \sum_{j_1, \dots, j_r \in \mathbbm{N}} j_ms(0, j_1, \dots, j_r), \quad u(t) \vcentcolon= \sum_{m = 1}^r u^m(0)G_m(t) + \int_0^t \rho(s) \mu G_1(t - s)ds.
\end{equation}

